<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco Franco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Generalísimo Franco</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco Franco was a Spanish general who rose in the colonial wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. In July 1936 he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armee-d-afrique">Army of Africa</a> in the military rising against the Second Republic and was named head of state of the Nationalist side. After winning the civil war in 1939 he ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as dictator until his death in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-franco.jpg" alt="Francisco Franco" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Franco_posed_portrait_photograph.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francisco Franco Bahamonde was born on 4 December 1892 in Ferrol, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, into a family that had given the Spanish Navy officers for six generations. He would have followed his father into the navy, but after the Spanish-American War the Naval Academy admitted no new entrants from 1906 to 1913. He entered the Infantry Academy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1907 and graduated in July 1910 as a second lieutenant. Serving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, where officers could win promotion on the battlefield, he became a brigadier general in 1926 at the age of 33.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A conservative and monarchist, Franco regretted the end of the monarchy in 1931 but did not challenge the Second Republic. He led the suppression of the 1934 uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> won the 1936 election he was sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>. He joined the military coup of July 1936 and took command of the 30,000 soldiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armee-d-afrique">Army of Africa</a>, securing the Moroccan protectorate in part by the summary execution of some 200 senior officers loyal to the Republic. With aircraft from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> he began an air bridge to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>.</p><p>Franco was proclaimed Generalísimo and head of state of the Nationalists in October 1936. On 19 April 1937 he merged the Falange and the Carlist parties into a single party. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> fell in January 1939 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 28 March, and he proclaimed victory on 1 April 1939. Repression followed. Paul Preston estimates that Franco's forces killed about 420,000 people in the war and afterwards, while Antony Beevor cites at least 35,000 official executions after 1939, and figures vary widely. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> stayed non-belligerent in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> though it supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>, and the economy grew rapidly from the late 1950s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1969 Franco named Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-carlos-i">Juan Carlos de Borbón</a> as his heir. He died on 20 November 1975, aged 82, and was entombed in the Valley of the Fallen; in 2018 the government approved legal changes that led to plans to exhume his remains. His legacy remains disputed. Scholars debate whether his regime should be called fascist, a military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> or something between, and Paul Preston and Stanley Payne hold differing views.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francisco Franco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 1 October 1936 the Junta de Defensa Nacional, which governed the territories the Nationalists held, recognised <b>Francisco Franco</b> as Caudillo of Spain, the Spanish equivalent of the Duce and the Führer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Condor Legion fought for <b>Franco</b> in the Spanish Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The surviving monarchies of western Europe adopted the British model, and Spain returned to monarchy under its constitution of 1978, after the dictatorship of <b>Francisco Franco</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Dictators also ruled Poland after Józef Piłsudski’s coup of 1926, Portugal under António de Oliveira Salazar, Spain under <b>Francisco Franco</b> from 1939, much of the Balkans and the Baltic states, and Brazil under Getúlio Vargas from 1937.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>…communist people’s republic in 1924, in Portugal’s Estado Novo, whose National Union was the only permitted political organisation, and in <b>Francisco Franco</b>’s Spain, where the movements supporting him were merged into a single party in 1937; in 1940 Japan’s parties dissolved themselves into the Imperial Rule Assistance Association.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Eduardo Marquina wrote words for Alfonso XIII's silver wedding anniversary, and José María Pemán wrote others in 1928, under Primo de Rivera; Pemán later changed his to carry the symbols of <b>Francisco Franco</b>'s regime, the raised-arm salute and the Falange's yoke and arrows.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ferrol, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.48,-8.23&amp;year=1945" aria-label="Ferrol on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4352 622 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francisco Franco"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4802 892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4802 892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.48,-8.23&amp;year=1945">What was here in 1945? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
